---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import CtaBand from "~/components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "~/data/site";
---
<Base
  title="About MyWebMaster — the studio behind Autopilot"
  description="MyWebMaster is a UK web engineering studio. We built Autopilot because website maintenance keeps sites alive without ever making them any better."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "About", href: "/about/" }]}
>
  <section class="section head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="label">About</p>
      <h1>We got tired of invoicing for maintenance that maintained nothing.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {site.name} is a UK web engineering studio. We have spent years building and looking
        after websites for small businesses, and the uncomfortable pattern was always the same.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>The problem we kept watching</h2>
      <p>
        A client pays for a site. It launches, and on that day it is the best it will ever be.
        Then a maintenance plan starts: updates get applied, backups run, uptime is monitored,
        and the invoices go out monthly. Three years later the site is perfectly maintained and
        performing worse than the day it launched, because nothing in that list was ever aimed
        at making it better.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meanwhile the work that would have helped — the broken link nobody noticed, the page
        title that has been costing clicks since 2021, the query the site is shown for four
        hundred times a month and answers nowhere — sits in a document from a paid audit that
        nobody had time to action.
      </p>
      <p>
        The bottleneck was never knowing what to do. It was the distance between knowing and
        doing, and that distance is exactly what software is good at closing.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we run it on ourselves first</h2>
      <p>
        We considered launching Autopilot with a portfolio of client results. We could not do it
        honestly: results take a measurement window to exist, and anything shown sooner would
        have been variance dressed as evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        So this domain is the test subject. The baseline was published before any work started —
        {" "}
        <a href="/proof/">18,001 impressions and 26 clicks over 90 days</a> — and every change
        since is in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, including the ones that fail.
        If the system does not work, this site is where it will be visible first.
      </p>

      <h2>What we believe</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A number without a control group is a story.</strong> Traffic moves for a
          hundred reasons. If you cannot separate your work from the weather, you are not
          measuring, you are narrating.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Autonomy needs a published boundary.</strong> An AI with unbounded write access
          to a business's website is a liability regardless of how good the model is. Ours is a
          <a href="/what-it-changes/">table anyone can read</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reverts should be as visible as wins.</strong> A changelog containing only
          successes tells you the log is curated, not that the system is good.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You should be able to leave.</strong> The site, the repository and the history
          are yours throughout. Lock-in is a substitute for being worth keeping.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where we are</h2>
      <p>
        We work remotely with clients across the United Kingdom. If you want to talk about
        whether this fits your site, the <a href="/contact/">quickest route is a free scan</a>:
        one full pass over your site and a ranked list of what we would fix, sent whether or not
        you buy anything.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand />
</Base>

<style>
  .head h1 { margin-block: 1rem 1.2rem; max-width: 20ch; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 2.5em; }
  .prose ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .prose li + li { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
  .prose a { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
